  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a generic molecular ratchet mechanism that converts chemical input into directed mechanical motion. At the top, a sawtooth wheel and pawl symbolize a ratchet: random or cyclical motion is rectified so the wheel turns preferentially in one direction.\n\nThe central part explains this idea with a particle moving on a periodically modulated energy landscape over repeated cycles. The wavy track has colored segments and local peaks and valleys, with arrows indicating how parts of the landscape shift. A green particle initially sits in one minimum. During a “balance breaking” step, the symmetry of the landscape is altered so one side becomes favored. This is followed by a “power stroke,” where the minimum effectively moves and the particle relaxes forward into a new position. Repeating these steps advances the particle by one period per cycle, despite the landscape remaining overall periodic.\n\nThe bottom panel summarizes the concept as two coupled chemical ratchet states, labeled Ratchet a and Ratchet b, plotted against chemical change and mechanical state. Transitions driven by fuel and waste connect the states, showing how a chemical reaction cycle biases otherwise reversible mechanical transitions to produce net directional motion.",
